== fuller page ==
Above floor 0 the library names four floors, and their names spell C4: 1
containers, 2 components, 3 configurations, 4 connections. Then comes 5,
running [floors]. C4.md lists "Containers Components Configurations
Connections" as one of four four-C readings of the name [fourc].

A second ladder uses the same numbers. sg1pl, a small tool that fetches a value
and checks it, climbs 0 read, 1 present, 2 fresh, 3 digest, 4
signed. Present: a real file you can read, not empty. Fresh: its time is not in
the future. Digest: a sha256 file beside it matches. Signed: a gpg signature
beside it verifies [locks]. Each level "does everything below it, then one more
thing" [below].

Corwin's ruling calls a move between the low levels a "tumbler change", and
says an application climbing above them is still "running" to the host
[tumblers]. An older note saw two ladders fighting over one digit; the ruling
closed that [twoladders].

This page hangs one lock on each floor's door: present on containers, fresh on
components, and so on up. That pairing is Mentat's reading of how his two
ladders fit. The sources share the numbers; none says floor 2 is the fresh
floor. It is his to bless [pairing].

Plainly, too: the floors are named, not yet tested. Every test in the library
sits at floor 0 [count]. And C4's language book names the climb another way
(configure, make, install, setup, self-test, enablement) [lang]; spread
10 sets the ladders side by side.

